\begin{abstract}
Self-Consistency (SC) improves large language model (LLM) reasoning by majority voting over chain-of-thought traces. A natural extension extracts formal logical constraints from traces and aggregates them with a symbolic solver, but we show this is a \emph{symbolic mirage}. We test this through Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation (SICA), which extracts first-order logic constraints under zero-shot prompting and solves weighted partial MAX-SAT. Self-extracted constraints systematically confirm their source trace's answer rather than providing independent verification, a phenomenon we term \emph{symbolic confirmation bias} (SCB). Formally, when nearly all constraints pass satisfiability checking, constraint-based aggregation degenerates to majority voting. This bias persists across nine models from 7B to frontier scale, with bias ratios of $1.66$--$4.97\times$. Twenty-seven training-free remediation strategies fail to break this bottleneck; a persistent independence--accuracy trade-off emerges where methods that reduce trace correlation also destroy accuracy. Among training-free approaches, only a structurally independent cross-architecture verifier restores corrective signal; the bottleneck lies in self-extraction itself, not model capability. We provide three zero-cost diagnostics that predict failure modes from a pilot run\footnote{Code and data are available at \url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/symb_invariant_consensus-2E1F/}.}.
\end{abstract}

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}
\label{sec:pipeline}
\label{sec:condorcet}
\label{sec:effective-k}

Self-Consistency (SC) \citep{wang2023sc} samples $K$ reasoning traces and selects the majority answer. Under correlated traces (Fleiss' $\kappa$ \citep{fleiss1971kappa}), the effective ensemble size $K_\text{eff}$ is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:effk}
K_{\text{eff}} = K \,/\, [1 + (K{-}1) \cdot \kappa]
\end{equation}
(following \citet{ladha1992correlated}). Intuitively, $K_\text{eff}$ measures how many effectively independent votes $K$ correlated traces contribute; e.g., $K{=}12$ traces with $\kappa{=}0.69$ yield $K_\text{eff} \approx 1.3$, meaning twelve correlated traces carry no more independent information than 1.3 uncorrelated ones. This motivates constraint-based aggregation.

Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation (SICA) extends SC by extracting formal constraints from traces and selecting the answer best supported by the satisfiable subset (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{f2_regime_scatter_v2}
\caption{SICA $\Delta$ (pp) vs.\ Fleiss' $\kappa$ (inter-trace agreement). Color encodes model family; shape encodes dataset (\scalebox{0.7}{$\bullet$}\,PW, \scalebox{0.7}{$\blacktriangle$}\,FOLIO, \scalebox{0.7}{$\blacksquare$}\,LogiQA). Error bars show multi-seed standard deviation. High $\kappa$ ($K_{\text{eff}} \to 1$) clusters near $\Delta \approx 0$; only the low-$\kappa$ region admits nonzero effects, consistent with the independence bottleneck.}
\label{fig:regime-scatter}
\end{figure*}

\paragraph{Trace generation.} Given question $q$, an LLM generates $K$ traces $\{t_1, \ldots, t_K\}$ at temperature $T = 0.7$. Each trace $t_i$ yields answer $a_i$.

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Constraint extraction.}\label{sec:constraint-extraction} The same LLM extracts logical constraints from each trace at $T_{\text{ext}} = 0.3$. The extraction prompt requests JSON output with constraint type, expression, and Z3-compatible formula (Appendix~\ref{app:prompts}); in practice, 99.0\% of extracted constraints are propositional at the Z3 level, with only 1.0\% using true quantifiers. The \emph{extraction rate} is the fraction of traces yielding at least one parseable constraint.

\paragraph{Deduplication and MAX-SAT.} Constraints are deduplicated by syntactic equivalence (global average deduplication ratio: 46.0\%); each unique constraint $c_m$ receives weight $w_m$ equal to the number of source traces. The objective finds the largest-weight satisfiable subset:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:maxsat}
\mathcal{C}^* = \arg\max_{\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{C}} \sum_{c \in \mathcal{S}} w_c \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathcal{S} \text{ is satisfiable.}
\end{equation}
Z3 \citep{demoura2008z3} solves this with a 10-second timeout (Algorithm~\ref{alg:maxsat}; Appendix~\ref{app:z3-pseudocode}). Answer assertion constraints ($\texttt{answer\_a} == \texttt{True}$, weight 1) ensure every candidate receives a nonzero base score; SICA is thus a strict generalization of SC.

\paragraph{Scoring.} Let $\mathcal{C}^{\setminus} = \mathcal{C} \setminus \mathcal{C}^*$. Each candidate $a$ is scored by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:score}
\text{score}(a) = \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^*} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a) - \alpha \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^{\setminus}} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(c, a) = 1$ if $c$ was extracted from any trace producing answer $a$, and $\alpha = 0.5$. The selected answer is $a^* = \arg\max_{a} \text{score}(a)$.

\paragraph{Degeneration condition.} If self-extracted constraints are coupled to their source answer, MAX-SAT retains nearly all ($|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$), and scoring degenerates to:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:degenerate}
\text{score}(a) \approx \sum_{\substack{c \in \mathcal{C}^* \\ \text{source}(c) \to a}} w_c,
\end{equation}
equivalent to majority voting. We show in \S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias} that this degeneration occurs in practice.

\paragraph{The $C_0$ diagnostic.} The multi-candidate rate $C_0 = |\{q : |\mathcal{A}(q)| > 1\}| / |\mathcal{Q}|$ is a zero-cost diagnostic: $C_0 < 5\%$ means SICA reduces to SC. $\alpha = 0.5$ is not critical (${\pm}0.49$\,pp across $\alpha \in [0, 1]$).

\paragraph{Bias ratio.} We quantify extraction bias via the \emph{bias ratio} (BR):
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mirage}
\text{BR}(M,T) = \frac{P(c \text{ supports } a_M \mid a_M \neq a^*)}{P(c \text{ supports } a^* \mid a_M \neq a^*)},
\end{equation}
where $c$ is a self-extracted constraint, $a_M$ the model's majority answer, and $a^*$ the ground truth. BR quantifies the degree of extraction degeneration under source-attribution scoring (Observation~1): values above 1 reflect the trace-count base rate inherent to source-attributed constraints. $\text{BR} = 1$ indicates no coupling beyond chance. We validate this metric empirically in \S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
\looseness=-1
Formal verification's value depends on the \emph{independence} of its inputs, not the soundness of its logic. SICA reduces to majority vote under source-attribution scoring (Observation~1), and 27 training-free variants fail to escape; symbolic confirmation bias holds across nine models (7B to frontier). Three zero-cost diagnostics predict failure from a $K{=}12$ pilot (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic-framework}).

\looseness=-1
For the broader neuro-symbolic community, our results expose a structural limitation of self-extraction pipelines: when the same model generates both reasoning and formal constraints, the symbolic layer inherits the model's distributional prior rather than providing an independent check. This limitation is not specific to logical constraint extraction; it applies to any self-verification paradigm where the verifier shares the generator's knowledge boundary \citep{huang2024selfcorrect}.

\looseness=-1
Cross-architecture verification escapes only when the verifier exceeds generator accuracy (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}); this points to \emph{architectural diversity} over scale as the key design principle. Trained verifiers---PRM \citep{lightman2024prm,wang2024mathshepherd}, ORM \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k}---succeed precisely because they break the $M{\to}T{\to}A{\to}C$ chain with independently supervised signal; our NLI escape instantiates this principle without task-specific training ($+6.67$\,pp). For test-time compute scaling \citep{snell2025scaling}, our findings imply that allocating additional inference budget to self-consistent sampling yields diminishing returns once $K_\text{eff}$ saturates; redirecting compute toward structurally independent verification offers a more efficient path. Lightweight trained verification is a natural next step (Appendix~\ref{app:extended-discussion}).

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Self-consistency and ensemble theory.}
Self-consistency (SC) selects the majority answer across chain-of-thought traces \citep{wang2023sc}, with extensions for free-form generation \citep{chen2024usc}, confidence weighting \citep{taubenfeld2025cisc}, diverse prompting \citep{li2023diverse}, and reasoning-aware filtering \citep{wan2024rasc, toh2024notallvotes, kim2026reasc}. SC gains plateau early \citep{loo2025reevaluating, byerly2025selfconsistency}. The Condorcet Jury Theorem \citep{condorcet1785} guarantees majority-vote convergence under independence; ensemble theory \citep{dietterich2000ensemble, krogh1994neural} requires error diversity. \citet{ladha1992correlated} show correlated voters reduce effective ensemble size; our $K_\text{eff}$ adapts Fleiss' $\kappa$ from diversity metrics \citep{kuncheva2003measures, brown2005diversity} to quantify this (\S\ref{sec:pipeline}). \citet{kim2025correlated} demonstrate correlated LLM errors; \citet{kuai2026independent} propose cross-model independence auditing.

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Neuro-symbolic reasoning.}
Three paradigms exist: \emph{specification-generation} \citep{pan2023logiclm, olausson2023linc, ye2024satlm} and \emph{program-generation} \citep{gao2023pal, chen2023program, lyu2023faithful} formalize before answer generation; \emph{trace-extraction} (this work) operates post-hoc, inheriting confirmation bias (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). Recent work extends symbolic planning and verified reasoning \citep{ryu2025clover, xu2026dylos, li2025aristotle, wang2025fover, singh2026verge, luo2025logicagent, miya2025eidoku}; \citet{amonkar2025realitycheck} show formalization quality degrades with complexity; \citet{chan2026position} argue logical soundness alone is unreliable. Prior studies note high formalization error rates but treat them as quality problems; we show they are \emph{systematically biased} toward the model's answer (Observation~1). This reveals a mechanism underlying \citet{huang2024selfcorrect}'s finding that LLMs cannot self-correct without external feedback: we formalize the failure via the $M{\to}T{\to}A{\to}C$ Markov chain and provide three zero-cost diagnostics that predict failure modes before deployment. Our symbolic confirmation bias (SCB) is distinct from CoT-level confirmation bias \citep{wan2025unveiling, turpin2023language, jhaveri2026failing}: CoT bias concerns trace-level faithfulness, while SCB shows the \emph{collection} of self-extracted constraints mirrors the answer distribution even when individual traces are locally faithful.

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Verification and test-time scaling.}
Scaling test-time compute via additional traces \citep{snell2025scaling}, structured search \citep{yao2023tree}, or extended reasoning \citep{guo2025deepseekr1, qwen2024qwq} faces the same correlation challenge when traces share a model's biases. Self-verification methods, including multi-agent debate \citep{liang2023encouraging, du2023improving}, self-evaluation \citep{xie2023selfevaluation, weng2023selfverification, madaan2023selfrefine, dhuliawala2023chainofverification}, iterative improvement \citep{shinn2023reflexion, zhou2023lats}, tool-augmented critique \citep{gou2024critic}, formal CoT verification \citep{zhu2025vericot, chen2025sets}, and multi-agent verification \citep{lifshitz2025mav}, report gains but none tests self-extracted constraints under formal solvers \citep[cf.][]{kamoi2024selfcorrect}; the bottleneck lies in constraint extraction, not verification (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). Trained verifiers can escape this bottleneck through external reward signals: outcome and process reward models \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k, lightman2024prm, wang2024mathshepherd}, discriminator-guided decoding \citep{khalifa2023grace}, RL-based self-verification \citep{zeng2025s2r}, dual preference optimization \citep{dupo2025}, and execution-based filtering \citep{li2022alphacode, chen2023codet}. These operate outside Observation~1's pipeline and may overcome the information-theoretic bottleneck. The failure modes differ: SICA's bottleneck is extraction fidelity, whereas PRM/ORM's is reward model generalization \citep{wang2024mathshepherd}.

